        <!-- Panel 1: Prepositions & Contractions -->
        <div class="ref-panel" id="refpanel-prepositions" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="reftab-prepositions">
          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-ochre">Fixed Case: Accusative</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Accusative Prepositions</h3>
            <p>These prepositions always govern the <strong>accusative case</strong> (only masculine articles change: <em>der &rarr; den</em>, <em>ein &rarr; einen</em>):</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>durch</strong> (through): <span class="example-chip">durch den Park</span></li>
              <li><strong>für</strong> (for): <span class="example-chip">für meinen Freund</span></li>
              <li><strong>gegen</strong> (against / around): <span class="example-chip">gegen den Plan</span></li>
              <li><strong>ohne</strong> (without): <span class="example-chip">ohne einen Schirm</span></li>
              <li><strong>um</strong> (around / at [time]): <span class="example-chip">um acht Uhr</span></li>
              <li><strong>bis</strong> (until / as far as): <span class="example-chip">bis nächsten Freitag</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-green">Fixed Case: Dative</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Dative Prepositions</h3>
            <p>These prepositions always require the <strong>dative case</strong> (<em>der/das &rarr; dem</em>, <em>die [sg.] &rarr; der</em>, <em>die [pl.] &rarr; den + -n</em>):</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>aus</strong> (from / out of): <span class="example-chip">aus dem Büro</span></li>
              <li><strong>bei</strong> (at / near / with): <span class="example-chip">beim Arzt</span></li>
              <li><strong>mit</strong> (with / by transport): <span class="example-chip">mit dem Zug</span></li>
              <li><strong>nach</strong> (after / to [cities, countries]): <span class="example-chip">nach dem Kurs</span></li>
              <li><strong>seit</strong> (since / for [time]): <span class="example-chip">seit einem Jahr</span></li>
              <li><strong>von</strong> (from / by): <span class="example-chip">vom Bahnhof</span></li>
              <li><strong>zu</strong> (to [people, places]): <span class="example-chip">zur Arbeit / zum Amt</span></li>
              <li><strong>gegenüber</strong> (opposite): <span class="example-chip">gegenüber der Post</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card is-wide">
            <span class="tag-green">Two-Way Rules</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Wechselpräpositionen (Accusative vs. Dative)</h3>
            <p>Nine spatial prepositions (<strong>an, auf, hinter, in, neben, über, unter, vor, zwischen</strong>) switch case depending on whether the verb expresses <strong>directed movement</strong> or <strong>static location</strong>:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="ref-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Question</th>
                    <th>Case</th>
                    <th>Typical Verbs</th>
                    <th>Example</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>Wohin?</strong> (Where to?)</td>
                    <td><strong>Accusative</strong> (movement / change of place)</td>
                    <td><em>gehen, fahren, stellen, legen, setzen, hängen</em></td>
                    <td>Ich gehe <span class="de-word">ins</span> Kino. / Er legt das Buch <span class="de-word">auf den</span> Tisch.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>Wo?</strong> (Where?)</td>
                    <td><strong>Dative</strong> (static position / location)</td>
                    <td><em>sein, bleiben, stehen, liegen, sitzen, warten, arbeiten</em></td>
                    <td>Ich bin <span class="de-word">im</span> Kino. / Das Buch liegt <span class="de-word">auf dem</span> Tisch.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card is-wide">
            <span class="tag-ochre">Complete Lookup Chart</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Standard German Prepositional Contractions</h3>
            <p>In everyday and written German, frequent preposition + definite article pairs fuse into a single word without an apostrophe:</p>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="ref-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Contraction</th>
                    <th>Components</th>
                    <th>Case &amp; Gender</th>
                    <th>Natural Usage Example</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">im</td><td>in + dem</td><td>Dative (masc. / neut.)</td><td>Wir warten <span class="de-word">im</span> Büro. / <span class="de-word">im</span> Januar</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">ins</td><td>in + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Sie geht heute Abend <span class="de-word">ins</span> Kino.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">am</td><td>an + dem</td><td>Dative (masc. / neut.)</td><td>Der Termin ist <span class="de-word">am</span> Montag. / <span class="de-word">am</span> Fenster</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">ans</td><td>an + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Er geht <span class="de-word">ans</span> Telefon. / Wir fahren <span class="de-word">ans</span> Meer.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">zum</td><td>zu + dem</td><td>Dative (masc. / neut.)</td><td>Ich fahre <span class="de-word">zum</span> Bahnhof. / <span class="de-word">zum</span> Arzt</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">zur</td><td>zu + der</td><td>Dative (feminine)</td><td>Sie geht zu Fuß <span class="de-word">zur</span> Post / <span class="de-word">zur</span> Schule.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">vom</td><td>von + dem</td><td>Dative (masc. / neut.)</td><td>Er kommt gerade <span class="de-word">vom</span> Rathaus / <span class="de-word">vom</span> Zahnarzt.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">beim</td><td>bei + dem</td><td>Dative (masc. / neut.)</td><td>Ich war gestern <span class="de-word">beim</span> Bäcker / <span class="de-word">beim</span> Friseur.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">aufs</td><td>auf + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Sie stellt die Tasse <span class="de-word">aufs</span> Regal.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">fürs</td><td>für + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Vielen Dank <span class="de-word">fürs</span> Zuhören!</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">durchs</td><td>durch + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Die Kinder laufen <span class="de-word">durchs</span> Haus.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">ums</td><td>um + das</td><td>Accusative (neuter)</td><td>Wir gehen einmal <span class="de-word">ums</span> Gebäude.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <!-- Panel 2: Separable Verbs & Satzklammer -->
        <div class="ref-panel" id="refpanel-separable" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="reftab-separable" hidden>
          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-green">Prefix Inventory</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Separable vs. Inseparable Prefixes</h3>
            <p>Stressed prefixes detach from the verb stem in main clauses; unstressed prefixes stay permanently attached.</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>Always separable (stressed):</strong> <em>ab-, an-, auf-, aus-, bei-, ein-, fern-, mit-, nach-, vor-, weg-, zu-, zurück-, zusammen-</em>.<br><span class="example-chip">abfahren &rarr; Der Zug fährt um 9 Uhr ab.</span></li>
              <li><strong>Always inseparable (unstressed):</strong> <em>be-, emp-, ent-, er-, ge-, miss-, ver-, zer-</em>.<br><span class="example-chip">verstehen &rarr; Ich verstehe den Text.</span></li>
              <li><strong>No <em>ge-</em> in Perfekt for inseparable verbs:</strong> <span class="example-chip">Ich habe das Formular bezahlt.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-ochre">Modal &amp; Clause Interactions</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">When Separable Verbs Stay Together</h3>
            <p>A separable verb only splits when it is the conjugated finite verb in a main clause:</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>With a modal verb:</strong> The modal sits in Position 2; the separable verb moves to the end in its <strong>full infinitive</strong>.<br><span class="example-chip">Sie müssen das Formular <strong>ausfüllen</strong>.</span></li>
              <li><strong>In subordinate clauses (<em>weil, dass, wenn</em>):</strong> The conjugated verb stays attached to its prefix at the very end.<br><span class="example-chip">..., weil der Bus pünktlich <strong>ankommt</strong>.</span></li>
              <li><strong>In the Imperative:</strong> Conjugated stem in Position 1, prefix at the end.<br><span class="example-chip">Füllen Sie bitte das Formular <strong>aus</strong>!</span></li>
              <li><strong>In the Perfekt tense:</strong> <em>-ge-</em> sits between prefix and participle.<br><span class="example-chip">ausfüllen &rarr; aus<strong>ge</strong>füllt</span> / <span class="example-chip">einziehen &rarr; ein<strong>ge</strong>zogen</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card is-wide">
            <span class="tag-green">Satzklammer Blueprint</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">The German Sentence Bracket (Satzklammer)</h3>
            <p>Position 2 holds the conjugated verb; the detached prefix, infinitive, or past participle closes the clause at the very end, enclosing time, manner, and objects inside the bracket.</p>
            <div class="bracket-diagram" aria-label="Sentence bracket structure">
              <div class="bracket-slot is-v2">
                <span class="bracket-caption">Position 2 (Left Bracket)</span>
                ruft / muss / hat
              </div>
              <div class="bracket-slot is-mid">
                <span class="bracket-caption">Mittelfeld (Time · Manner · Place · Objects)</span>
                am Nachmittag seine Kollegin im Büro
              </div>
              <div class="bracket-slot is-end">
                <span class="bracket-caption">End Position (Right Bracket)</span>
                an / anrufen / angerufen
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <!-- Panel 3: Conjunctions & Word Order -->
        <div class="ref-panel" id="refpanel-conjunctions" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="reftab-conjunctions" hidden>
          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-green">Position 0</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Coordinating Conjunctions (ADUSO)</h3>
            <p><strong>A</strong>ber, <strong>D</strong>enn, <strong>U</strong>nd, <strong>S</strong>ondern, <strong>O</strong>der link two main clauses at <strong>Position 0</strong> without altering standard subject–verb word order:</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>und</strong> (and) · <strong>oder</strong> (or) · <strong>aber</strong> (but / however)</li>
              <li><strong>denn</strong> (because — main clause order: verb stays in Position 2)</li>
              <li><strong>sondern</strong> (but rather — used only after a negative statement)</li>
              <li><span class="example-chip">Er bleibt zu Hause, <strong>denn</strong> er <strong>hat</strong> Fieber.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card">
            <span class="tag-ochre">Verb at End</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Subordinating Conjunctions</h3>
            <p>Subordinating conjunctions require a preceding comma and send the <strong>conjugated finite verb to the very end</strong> of the dependent clause:</p>
            <ul class="rule-list">
              <li><strong>weil / da</strong> (because / since), <strong>obwohl</strong> (although), <strong>dass</strong> (that), <strong>ob</strong> (whether)</li>
              <li><strong>wenn</strong> (if / whenever / present or repeated past), <strong>als</strong> (when — single completed event in the past)</li>
              <li><strong>nachdem</strong> (after), <strong>bevor</strong> (before), <strong>während</strong> (while)</li>
              <li><span class="example-chip">Ich bleibe hier, <strong>weil</strong> ich heute noch arbeiten <strong>muss</strong>.</span></li>
              <li><strong>Fronted clause rule:</strong> If the subordinate clause comes first, the main clause starts immediately with its verb: <span class="example-chip">Wenn es regnet, <strong>bleiben</strong> wir drinnen.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="ref-card is-wide">
            <span class="tag-green">Position 1 + Inversion</span>
            <h3 class="ref-card-title">Adverbial Connectors &amp; Subject–Verb Inversion</h3>
            <p>Conjunctive adverbs occupy <strong>Position 1</strong> (or sit in the Mittelfeld). When placed at Position 1, the conjugated verb must stay in <strong>Position 2</strong>, forcing the subject directly behind it (Subject–Verb inversion).</p>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="ref-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Connector</th>
                    <th>Meaning</th>
                    <th>Word Order Pattern (Pos. 1 &rarr; Pos. 2 Verb &rarr; Subject)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">deshalb / deswegen</td><td>therefore / that is why</td><td>Es regnet stark. <span class="de-word">Deshalb nehmen</span> wir die U-Bahn.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">trotzdem</td><td>nevertheless / despite that</td><td>Der Bus hat Verspätung. <span class="de-word">Trotzdem kommen</span> wir pünktlich.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">dann / danach</td><td>then / afterwards</td><td>Ich fülle das Formular aus. <span class="de-word">Danach gebe</span> ich es ab.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">außerdem</td><td>moreover / besides</td><td>Die Wohnung ist hell. <span class="de-word">Außerdem liegt</span> sie zentral.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="de-word">sonst</td><td>otherwise</td><td>Beeil dich, <span class="de-word">sonst verpassen</span> wir den Zug!</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p style="margin-top: var(--space-sm);"><strong>TEKAMOLO Reminder:</strong> Inside the Mittelfeld, adverbials follow <strong>Te</strong>mporal (when?) &rarr; <strong>Ka</strong>usal (why?) &rarr; <strong>Mo</strong>dal (how?) &rarr; <strong>Lo</strong>kal (where?): <span class="example-chip">Wir fahren <em>morgen</em> (T) <em>wegen des Termins</em> (K) <em>mit dem Zug</em> (M) <em>nach Köln</em> (L).</span></p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

